A||||8||Scott2M4||Steps/tips to changing a 2.5l serpentine belt||||5
Z||000000||Scott2M4||11-04-1999||04:48 PM||||My mechanic just told me my serpentine belt is showing severe signs of wear ("I'm sorry, could you repeat that?  I couldn't hear you over that God-awful squealing noise coming from the rear of the car!!!!!")   ;-)<P>Anyway, even though I've never changed a belt before, I thought I'd give it a try.  Besides, he was going to charge me $100 to do the work.<P>So, I'm looking for tips/suggestions/cautions and basically the steps and tools needed to change one.  I checked my Chilton's manual but didn't find anything on changing one.  I checked the archives for info on this.  Saw discussions regarding the tensioner (which he claimed is fine on mine) but nothing in the way of the belt itself.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.  I'd like to try and tackle this tonight.  Warming the car up before I go to work at 4AM is starting to tick the neighbors off!   ;-)<P>I'll check back in a couple of hours.  I'm heading out to Pep Boys to buy the belt.  Thanks, folks!  Oh, BTW, in case it makes a difference, I have A/C.||||reg||5
Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-04-1999||05:58 PM||||Serpentine belts are usually pretty easy!  Just pay close attention to the way you take it off.  There should be a sticker somewhere with a diagram of how the belt is routed.  If there isn't you might want to take a moment to draw one real quick.  Then all you have to do is put a wrench to the bolt in the middle of the tensioner and push (or pull, which ever way loosens it)!  Make sure that when you have the belt off that the tensioner moves back and forth freely and that it turns freely also.  I don't know why it would squeal unless it was sticking.  Also make sure that it isn't you alternator that is making the noise!!||||reg||4
Z||000002||Gokart||11-04-1999||07:36 PM||||I only had my car a week before the belt wore off. I had to keep stopping and letting it cool and going again till I got it home. All I needed to do to get the new belt on was loosen the alternator, put it on, then pull back on the alternator, wedging it against something with a long crow bar, then tightened the alternator back again. Had to tighten it a little more a few weeks later the same way when the new belt broke itself in a little. Not a problem since! Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Scott2M4||11-04-1999||08:42 PM||||I've heard a loose belt will only squeal like the one it replaced and a tight belt will wear out bearings.<P>Is there a way to gauge the proper tension?  I've heard +/- 1/2" travel in either direction - is this correct?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Eddie||11-04-1999||08:56 PM||||Scott<BR>	I Think that RZURCTN IS RIGHT.I had an alternator go bad on my old cavlier it was squealing like a pig .it had bad bearing it cant hurt to take a look hay good luck any way. <BR>			EDDIE||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||11-04-1999||09:24 PM||||<BR><B>$100 to change a serpentine belt ??!!!</B><P>Someone's yanking your chain. It is a two minute job. Put a socket wrench on the tensioner pulley bolt and turn it <B>clockwise</B> to loosen the belt tension. The Haynes manual gives the WRONG direction.<P>The fellas here who are talking about adjusting the belt tension and prying against the alternator are NOT talking about a serpentine belt system. There is NO adjustment to be made.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000006||theogre||11-05-1999||04:30 PM||||The belt tensioner uses a 15mm socket.<P>look at your present belt carfully. Make sure its running straight over the tensioner and all other pullys. If the belt is running off one side of the tensioner then likely the tensioner is failing. This unit must be replaced as a whole part. You can't service it. Do Not attempt to remove the pivot bolt from the center of the thing. The tensioner spring is under load at all times. even with the belt off.<P>The new belt fits only one way. It is a bit tricky to get it on all the pullys at the same time then hold it while you slip it back on the tensioner. It tends to be easier with 2 people. one person fooling with the top, then the other holds the belt in the bottom pulleys while the top guy puts it on the tensioner.<P>There is no real method. whatever gets it on there. And the tensioner releases when rotated clockwise. I never looked in the book for that.......||||reg||1
Z||000007||RZURCTN||11-06-1999||12:08 AM||||The only reason for a serpentine belt to squeal is if there is a problem with the tensioner.  Like all things mechanical, it is bound to wear out at some point.  You don't have to worry about proper adjustment with a tensioner that is working properly.  It puts a predetermined load on the belt.  Just ensure that it functions right and all will be well.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-06-1999||12:55 PM||||RZ,<P>A serpent belt most definatly will squalk, even if the tensioner is good. All it takes is a little oil or grease on these to ruin them.<P>I think the rated life on the serpents is around 40-50Kmiles, much higher than V belts, but if they get contaminated they likely won't make 20K.<P>If you get grease or oil on a serpent clean it off, and make sure the pulleys are clean to. The citrus based cleaners should work well. Dawn dish soap is good to, at least for oil. Solvents can trash the belt so don't clean it in solvent.||||reg||1
